# Tap Python SDK

When the user wants to build something with a Tap device, read `.cursor/skills/tap-getting-started/SKILL.md` first.

- Install: `pip install tap-python-sdk`. Import: `from tapsdk import connect`. Do not `pip install tapsdk`.
- Order: `await connect()`, then `register_*` callbacks, then `await sdk.start()`, then enable input, then keep the loop alive. `connect()` alone delivers no taps.
- `connect()` returns `TapSDK` or `TapSDK2` from firmware. Do not call these “SDK v1” / “SDK v2”. Handle both unless the user names the device.
- If `hasattr(sdk, "set_input_mode")`, call `set_input_mode(InputModeController())` to get taps. Otherwise call `set_feature(DeviceFeatures.MODEL_DETECTION, True)` plus a vision model.
- Tapcode may be `int` or `[int]`. Normalize with `v[0] if isinstance(v, (list, tuple)) else v`.
- Callbacks run on the Bluetooth path: keep them short, never block, push work to an `asyncio.Queue`.
- Knob and D-Pad recipes need the TapXR developer firmware or TapBand (`tap-knob`, `tap-dpad`).
- TapBand uses the same BLE API as TapXR and is coming soon; it is not for sale yet.
- Do not invent APIs or enum values. Test with the real device.
